-- An @nlg that belongs to no node is not attached, and says so, after a rule
-- written as clauses as after any other rule. The CONSIDERs a group of clauses
-- is lowered to hold names that the parser made up; they used to take such an
-- @nlg without a word (refutation of legalese/l4-ide#545's review, round 4).
-- And an @nlg inside a WHERE of a clause is not written between two clauses,
-- so no later clause takes it. It is not attached either: a group's clause
-- does not yet give the definitions in its WHERE their @nlg, which a rule
-- written as one definition does (known, and unchanged).
DECLARE Colour IS ONE OF Red, Green

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE one 0 IS g 1
  WHERE
    @nlg the helper of the first clause %x%
    g x MEANS x + 1
DECIDE one other IS 2

GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE two Red IS 1
DECIDE two Green IS 2
@nlg after the last rule of the file
